 "claims": [
  {
   "id": "E_escape",
   "type": "established",
   "nature": "fact",
   "text": "Escobar escaped from La Catedral prison (near Medellín) on 22 July 1992 as the army moved to transfer him; the DEA history says 28 guards were later charged with aiding the break-out. Narration: 'escaped prison'.",
   "sources": [
    "dea_history",
    "dea_museum",
    "wiki_catedral",
    "ci_uk"
   ]
  },
  {
   "id": "E_hunt16",
   "type": "established",
   "nature": "fact",
   "text": "He was hunted for 16 months (July 1992 - December 1993; AP, quoting President Gaviria: 'hunted the drug trafficker for 16 months') by the Search Bloc, a Colombian National Police unit led by Col. Hugo Martínez, backed by US agencies; the DEA Museum calls it the largest manhunt in Colombia's history. Narration: 'hid for 16 months' / 'Colombia's Search Bloc hunted him'.",
   "sources": [
    "seattle_ap_1993",
    "deseret_ap_1993",
    "wiki_searchbloc",
    "ci_uk",
    "dea_museum",
    "britannica_caught"
   ]
  },
  {
   "id": "E_hook",
   "type": "established",
   "nature": "fact",
   "text": "On 2 December 1993 a phone call to his son, traced by police direction-finding, led the Search Bloc to the house in Medellín where he was killed the same day (Time: 'long enough for the Colombian police to trace the call'; Britannica: the Search Bloc 'traced a phone call'; the St. Petersburg Times headline: 'Final error: a phone call'). Headline: 'A PHONE CALL TO HIS SON / ENDED THE HUNT FOR ESCOBAR'.",
   "sources": [
    "time_1993",
    "time_son",
    "britannica_caught",
    "cnn_kp_2000",
    "pbs_frontline",
    "tampabay_call"
   ]
  },
  {
   "id": "E_avianca",
   "type": "established",
   "nature": "fact",
   "text": "Avianca Flight 203, a Boeing 727 from Bogotá to Cali, exploded and crashed on 27 November 1989, killing all 107 aboard; Colombian officials concluded a bomb caused it; the Medellín cartel's 'Extraditables' claimed it and Escobar was blamed; a US grand jury indicted Escobar in 1992 and the cartel hitman charged with the bombing was tried in New York in 1994 (not named on screen). Narration: 'His cartel bombed a jet, killing 107'.",
   "sources": [
    "wapo_avianca",
    "upi_avianca",
    "wapo_avianca_bomb",
    "upi_indict",
    "upi_avianca_jury",
    "cnn_bombings"
   ]
  },
  {
   "id": "E_bounty",
   "type": "established",
   "nature": "fact",
   "text": "The cartel offered money for each police officer killed (UPI, 1990: about $4,200 per officer; La FM: one to three million pesos by rank); by December 1993 Escobar and his gang were blamed for the deaths of at least 500 police officers in Medellín since 1989 (AP). Narration: 'paid bounties on police'. Card: '500+ OFFICERS KILLED SINCE 1989'.",
   "sources": [
    "upi_police_1990",
    "upi_carbomb_1990",
    "lafm_bounty",
    "seattle_ap_1993",
    "deseret_2012"
   ]
  },
  {
   "id": "E_us",
   "type": "established",
   "nature": "fact",
   "text": "The DEA assisted the Colombian National Police in the pursuit; the DEA history says his location was determined with 'electronic, directional-finding equipment'; Time: a scanning operation set up outside Medellín 'with U.S.-donated equipment'; Frontline: 'radio directional-finding equipment'; US signals teams worked with the Search Bloc (Wikipedia). Narration: 'with DEA help and US tracking gear'.",
   "sources": [
    "dea_history",
    "time_1993",
    "pbs_frontline",
    "wiki_searchbloc"
   ]
  },
  {
   "id": "E_radiotel",
   "type": "established",
   "nature": "fact",
   "text": "In hiding he kept calling his family (his wife and, more often, his son) on a radiotelephone, which gave the direction-finding teams signals to find (Frontline; St. Petersburg Times: he 'could not resist the temptation of speaking to his family in Bogotá'). Narration: 'he kept phoning his family on a radiotelephone'.",
   "sources": [
    "pbs_frontline",
    "tampabay_call",
    "historycollection",
    "time_son"
   ]
  },
  {
   "id": "E_son_plea",
   "type": "established",
   "nature": "fact",
   "text": "ATTRIBUTED (his son's account, CNN 2016): the son, then Juan Pablo Escobar, says he told his father 'Papa, don't call anymore. They are going to kill you.' Narration: 'His son says he begged him: stop calling, they will kill you.' He is referred to only as 'his son' (he later changed his name).",
   "sources": [
    "cnn_son_2016",
    "time_son",
    "cnn_kp_2000"
   ]
  },
  {
   "id": "E_call",
   "type": "established",
   "nature": "fact",
   "text": "On 2 December 1993 Escobar phoned his son (16, in Bogotá); he kept the first call short and promised to call back; the second call was his last and was long enough to trace. Col. Hugo Martínez (CNN transcript): 'when the communication started, we begin looking for him, and in about five minutes it was done. We knew exactly where he was.' Time: the calls were traced 'within 90 minutes' through the scanning operation. Narration: 'His first call was short. The second was long enough to trace.' Card: 'SIGNAL LOCKED IN ABOUT 5 MINUTES'.",
   "sources": [
    "cnn_kp_2000",
    "time_1993",
    "time_son",
    "pbs_frontline",
    "britannica_locate"
   ]
  },
  {
   "id": "E_house",
   "type": "established",
   "nature": "fact",
   "text": "The signal was pinpointed to a middle-class two-storey house in the western part of Medellín (Time; The Washington Post); secondary sources name the neighbourhood as Los Olivos. Card: 'A TWO-STOREY HOUSE · LOS OLIVOS, MEDELLÍN'. The drawn house and street are generic.",
   "sources": [
    "time_1993",
    "wapo_1993",
    "wiki_searchbloc",
    "historycollection"
   ]
  },
  {
   "id": "E_death",
   "type": "established",
   "nature": "fact",
   "text": "Police raided the house; Escobar and his bodyguard tried to flee across the roofs of adjoining houses and were shot dead by the Colombian National Police (DEA history; Britannica; The Washington Post; UPI). Narration: 'Police raided the house. He fled onto the roof and was shot dead.' Summary only; nothing of it is drawn.",
   "sources": [
    "dea_history",
    "britannica_die",
    "wapo_1993",
    "upi_1993",
    "time_1993"
   ]
  },
  {
   "id": "E_dispute",
   "type": "established",
   "nature": "fact",
   "text": "CARD ONLY: who fired the fatal shot is still disputed: Britannica notes 'uncertainty about who fired the fatal shot'; police accounts name different officers; Los Pepes figures (Carlos Castaño; 'Don Berna', who credited his brother) claimed it; his son believes from the forensic report that his father, badly wounded, shot himself. Card: 'WHO FIRED THE FATAL SHOT IS STILL DISPUTED'.",
   "sources": [
    "britannica_die",
    "aetv",
    "colombiareports_berna",
    "cnn_son_2016",
    "yahoo_bi"
   ]
  },
  {
   "id": "E_birthday",
   "type": "established",
   "nature": "fact",
   "text": "Escobar was born on 1 December 1949; 1 December 1993 was his 44th birthday (Britannica), and he was killed the next day, 2 December 1993, aged 44. Narration: 'The hunt ended the day after his 44th birthday.'",
   "sources": [
    "wiki_escobar",
    "britannica_locate",
    "wiki_death",
    "upi_onthisday"
   ]
  },
  {
   "id": "E_toll",
   "type": "established",
   "nature": "fact",
   "text": "MEMORIAL (card and last line): Escobar was accused of ordering the assassinations of presidential candidates, judges, journalists and police; hundreds of Colombians died in bombings of shopping centres, neighbourhoods and a passenger airplane (AP); the DEA history: the cartel was responsible for the assassinations of dozens of government officials. No reputable extract gives a verified total, so narration says 'everyone his cartel killed', not a number.",
   "sources": [
    "seattle_ap_1993",
    "dea_history",
    "csm_1990",
    "britannica_rise"
   ]
  }
 ],
